{
  "name": "Classify Mixed Documents and Route Invoices to Google Sheets with LDX hub AnalyzeDoc",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## How it works\n\nUpload any business document (PDF, JPEG, or PNG). A **low-detail** AnalyzeDoc pass classifies it — invoice, receipt, business card, contract, blank, or other — then a Switch routes the file by type.\n\nLow detail costs roughly 1/5–1/10 of a high-detail read. It reads titles and layout reliably, but not fine print — so classify cheap first, and spend high-detail credits only where extraction is actually needed.\n\nThe **invoice route is fully wired**: the original file is re-attached and sent through a high-detail extraction that pulls 12 key fields and appends them as one row to Google Sheets. The other routes are placeholders for your own flows.\n\n### Setup steps\n\n1. Get a free API key at gw.portal.ldxhub.io (25,000 credits/month, no credit card).\n2. Create an LDXhub API credential and select it in **both** AnalyzeDoc nodes.\n3. Connect Google Sheets credentials and pick a spreadsheet whose first row has these headers: invoice_number, issue_date, due_date, vendor_name, vendor_address, vendor_tax_id, bill_to, subtotal, tax, total, currency, payment_terms.\n4. In the Sheets node, after picking the sheet, set Mapping Column Mode back to **Map Automatically** — n8n switches it to manual whenever a sheet is selected.\n5. Run the workflow and upload a document.\n\n### Customization\n\n- Add or rename document types in both the classification prompt and the Switch rules.\n- If your document types share nearly identical layouts, add \"identify the type from the document title\" to the classification prompt.\n- Replace the No-Op placeholders with your own processing (OCR conversion, filing, notifications).",
        "width": 500,
        "height": 1120
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        -660,
        -480
      ],
      "id": "906856cd-b673-4245-9232-dca6e9e28584",
      "name": "Sticky Note"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Classify cheaply\n\nA low-detail vision pass assigns one of six document types. Ideal as a sorting gate for mixed scan folders.",
        "width": 460,
        "height": 340,
        "color": 7
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        160,
        -180
      ],
      "id": "71c2263d-43d6-4044-9a1c-70296663e964",
      "name": "Sticky Note1"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Invoice route (fully wired)\n\nThe first Code node re-attaches the original upload (binary data does not survive the parse step). After the **high-detail** extraction, a second Code node lifts the parsed fields from `data` to the top level so Google Sheets auto-mapping matches your column headers — just pick your spreadsheet.\n\nTip: keep example amounts as decimals (e.g. 1234.56) so they stay numeric.",
        "width": 1200,
        "height": 460,
        "color": 7
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        880,
        -520
      ],
      "id": "9e53534d-f0ba-4421-aa8d-f0af3cce2d11",
      "name": "Sticky Note2"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Your routes\n\nreceipt and business_card land here; everything else falls through to **other**. Replace the No-Ops with your own flows.",
        "width": 360,
        "height": 660,
        "color": 7
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        880,
        40
      ],
      "id": "a2a27694-dd92-4575-be31-2ec229e182bf",
      "name": "Sticky Note3"
    },
    {
